WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.983
- Hey, what's up, y'all.

00:00:00.983 --> 00:00:02.872
We're back and Seth Rogan is here with us.

00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:04.189
Give it up for Seth.

00:00:04.189 --> 00:00:05.317
(audience cheers)

00:00:05.317 --> 00:00:09.700
So Seth's new book, Year
Book is full of epic stories.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.010
And from time to time on the show,

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.060
we get guests with stories.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.680
So epic, so long,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.720
there's just not enough time to tell them.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.640
That ends right now.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.890
Today people, you're here for this.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.080
You're welcome.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.323
It's time for a new segment
called Epic Story Time.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.100
♪ Epic story time ♪

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.800
The deal is I asked one question,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.300
and Seth has a whole segment,

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.870
and a bunch of illustrations
to tell a story.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.030
I'm sure I'll have some follow-up
questions at the end, Seth

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.000
So, but here we go.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.040
Here's my one question.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.530
Hey, Seth, it's almost summer.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.280
Did you go to camp as a kid?

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.113
(Seth laughs)

00:00:42.113 --> 00:00:45.489
- I did go to summer camp,
as a kid, Kelly Clarkson.

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:47.180
(Seth and Kelly laugh)

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.350
And yes, I went to Jewish summer camp.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.170
I'm a Jewish person.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:52.003
A lot of Jews,

00:00:52.003 --> 00:00:53.540
go to a summer camp.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.220
And at my particular summer camp,

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.170
something to note, is that the
counselors were very young.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:03.410
The campers were around, you
know, between nine and 15.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.370
And the counselors were between like 17

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.960
and 20, maybe 19.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.960
So it's kinda like leaving
your dog in the care of a bird.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.890
They were not very responsible, basically.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.350
So every summer we would do
what was called a Teyule,

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.160
which was an overnight camping
trip away from the camp.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.620
And this time we were going to
hike the Juan de Fuca trail,

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.860
which is a 29 mile hike,

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.420
over the course of three
days and camp along the way.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.900
And the hike runs along
the mountainous coast of

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.100
of Vancouver Island, which
is in British Columbia.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.000
Exactly halfway through
the second day of the hike,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.490
so 14 miles into this hike,

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.540
a girl in our group

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.670
fell

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.270
and twisted her ankle severely,

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.840
to the point that she
passed out unconscious.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.170
So we're like

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:01.003
15,

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.040
15 year olds with,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.150
around three 17 and 18 year
olds who are our counselors,

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.553
now dealing with this,
they don't know what to do.

00:02:11.553 --> 00:02:13.000
We don't have cell phones at this point.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.390
They don't exist yet.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.360
So someone hands me an ax

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.540
and tells me to chop down a tree

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.540
so we could start
constructing a stretcher.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.200
Meanwhile, we're getting
cold and it's pouring rain,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.930
and the sun is starting to set.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.520
So all the girls in the group
start burning their tampons

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.780
because it's the only flammable dry thing.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.030
we have to create a fire.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.750
We finally make a
stretcher, me and my friend,

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.000
and we load this girl onto it.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.170
And the plan is to carry her 14 miles

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:45.930
back to where we started.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.360
After around three steps,
our stretcher breaks.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.560
And we dropped of this
poor girl in the mud.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.879
And we arguably make
her physical situation

00:02:55.879 --> 00:02:57.663
much, much, much worse.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.980
At point I'm handed a signal flare,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.430
which means things are getting bad.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.130
Once flares are coming out,

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.110
you know you're in a bad situation.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:09.580
We were standing on the coast

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.250
and I was excited to use the flare.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.050
And I saw a boat in the distance

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.630
and I pulled it and the
flare literally went one foot

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.650
and then fizzled down into the ocean.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.220
So that was not how we
were going to be rescued.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.210
Eventually, thank God,

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:25.450
three firemen, who happened
to be hiking the same trail,

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.400
found us and brought us to a
logging road that was nearby.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.170
The problem was there was 15 of us

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.080
and they had no way to
get all of us to safety.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.430
Some kids were getting
hypothermia at this point.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.670
We'd been missing for quite a while.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:38.600
- What?

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.520
- Though we, me and one other guy,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.140
and one of the fire men

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.870
went to a parking lot to start a big fire,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.560
so all the kids could get warm

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:49.823
that were nearby.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:54.360
But this fire man was very
inexperienced and young,

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.760
again, almost in our age,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.580
maybe his early twenties
and as he was lightening it,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.330
the gas can he was lighting it
with itself, caught on fire.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:07.620
And I had to kick it
across this parking lot,

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.790
essentially engulfing this
entire parking lot in flames

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.180
Eventually,

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.010
we were rescued.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:16.720
We went to the firehouse nearby

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.300
on this little town of Vancouver Island.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.150
They gave us wool blankets
and soup and things like that.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:26.010
And honestly, I saw one
of my counselors recently

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:30.550
and I told him that this
story was in the book

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.770
and I always viewed it
as such a funny story,

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.390
did he view it the same way,

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:38.540
to which he said that was the
worst day of my entire life.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:42.020
There's absolutely nothing
funny about that story.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.917
I've never been more scared of
anything ever than that day.

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:45.750
(Seth laughs)

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.500
So we remember it differently,

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.773
but in my head it was
hilarious, delightful event.

00:04:51.773 --> 00:04:53.943
(Seth and Kelly laugh)

00:04:53.943 --> 00:04:54.776
- What?

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.290
Okay, wait, I had so
many questions during it,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.343
but I didn't want to interject.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.730
Okay, here's my big question.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.260
When is this movie being made?

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.460
- Yeah, I don't know.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.900
I don't know if Lost Jews
in the Woods, is yeah,

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.140
coming out anytime soon.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:10.973
But maybe.

00:05:10.973 --> 00:05:13.210
- Well maybe, don't go with that title.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.773
But I'm just saying, I
would watch this movie.

00:05:15.773 --> 00:05:18.138
- From the people who brought
you 40 year old Virgin.

00:05:18.138 --> 00:05:18.971
It's a very,

00:05:18.971 --> 00:05:19.804
it's a similar title.

00:05:19.804 --> 00:05:20.690
(Seth and Kelly laugh)

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:21.523
- I just love it.

00:05:21.523 --> 00:05:24.067
It's like everything kept
going wrong in every..

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.110
- Oh yeah, we were on the news.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:27.943
It was a...

00:05:27.943 --> 00:05:29.240
- Oh my gosh.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:30.970
First of all, here's another question.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.810
How awesome did you feel

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.900
that they handed the ax
to you to chop down tree?

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:36.733
- I felt good.

00:05:36.733 --> 00:05:38.440
I was just a big kid.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:39.840
And I think with kids,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.960
you associate size with
intelligence, sometimes.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.390
Like I was, I,

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:45.380
I was large.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:46.880
and so I think someone was like

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.010
he's must be the most
capable in this situation,

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:50.373
but that was not true, at all.

00:05:50.373 --> 00:05:52.034
(Seth and Kelly laugh)

00:05:52.034 --> 00:05:53.510
- Oh my gosh.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:56.110
Well, I want to say thank you to Marcel.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:56.943
He's on our staff.

00:05:56.943 --> 00:05:58.220
He actually did those illustrations.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.053
Those were amazing.

00:05:59.053 --> 00:05:59.886
- They're amazing.

00:05:59.886 --> 00:06:00.719
Thank you.

00:06:00.719 --> 00:06:01.552
- Way to go, Marcel.

00:06:01.552 --> 00:06:02.400
I love you.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.360
Well, Seth Rogan, everybody

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.953
his new book is called
Yearbook and it's out now.

